<title>SOUTH AFRICA: SEA HARVEST RAISES YR ATTRIB PROFIT 17 PCT.</title>
<headline>SEA HARVEST RAISES YR ATTRIB PROFIT 17 PCT.</headline>
<dateline>JOHANNESBURG 1996-11-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year ended Sept 30</p>
<p>			(million rand unless stated)	 </p>
<p>    Shr, attrib (cts)    57.1	  vs   49.1</p>
<p>    Shr, headline (cts)  54.7	  vs   48.9</p>
<p>    Div (cts)		17.0 making 26.0 vs 23.0  </p>
<p>    Attrib income	   57.58	 vs  49.09    </p>
<p>    Pre-tax		   98.37	 vs  88.12</p>
<p>    Tax			 38.07	 vs  39.04</p>
<p>    Net investment income 14.04	 vs  12.68</p>
<p>    Turnover		 418.46	 vs 360.92</p>
<p>Div payable Jan 7</p>
<p>on register Dec 6</p>
<p>Full name: Sea Harvest Corp Ltd </p>
<p>    Share of associates'  2.73 loss   vs  -  </p>
<p>NOTE - The company said good winter catches and favourable</p>
<p>trading conditions during the second half, supported by improved</p>
<p>operating efficiencies and a lower tax rate, lifted attributable</p>
<p>earnings 17 percent.</p>
<p>It said that good progress was made in reducing the average</p>
<p>age of the fleet with the acquisition of three modern stern</p>
<p>trawlers.</p>
<p>Sea Harvest said that it anticipated the markets in which it</p>
<p>operates to remain firm in the coming financial year. Providing</p>
<p>catch rates in South Africa and Namibia are satisfactory and the</p>
<p>group's present quotas are maintained, real growth in earnings</p>
<p>could be expected, it added.</p>
<p>-- Johannesburg newsroom +27 11 482 1003</p>
</text>
